# ============================================================
# ORCHESTRATION GUARDRAILS ENGINE — SUPPORT DOCUMENT
# ============================================================
# Classification: INTERNAL — TRUSTED ENGINEERS ONLY
# System: Ardhanarishvara Infrastructure Layer
# Module: Orchestration Guardrails Engine (Y‑Prime Edition)
#
# This document describes the purpose, behavior, and safety
# responsibilities of the Orchestration Guardrails Engine.
# It is written in shell‑style format for compatibility with
# system scripts, installers, and internal tooling.
# ============================================================


# ============================================================
# SECTION 1 — OVERVIEW
# ============================================================
# The Orchestration Guardrails Engine is the supervisory safety
# layer that prevents runaway autonomous behavior inside the
# Ardhanarishvara ecosystem.
#
# It monitors:
#   - module-to-module interactions
#   - repeated communication loops
#   - task age (TTL)
#   - deadlock patterns
#
# It enforces:
#   - loop detection thresholds
#   - TTL expiration
#   - forced task termination
#
# All AI entities (Arka, Astra, Aruhan, Arkastra, Lux, Disturition)
# rely on this engine to maintain deterministic orchestration safety.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 2 — PURPOSE & RESPONSIBILITIES
# ============================================================
# PRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES:
#
#   1. Track module-to-module interactions.
#      - Every time Module A communicates with Module B, the engine
#        increments an interaction counter.
#
#   2. Register autonomous tasks with timestamps.
#      - Each task receives a creation timestamp.
#      - Used to enforce TTL (Time-To-Live).
#
#   3. Maintain active task counts.
#      - Prevents runaway task spawning.
#
#   4. Provide data for loop detection.
#      - Detects repeated cycles between modules.
#
#   5. Provide data for TTL enforcement.
#      - Flags tasks that exceed their allowed lifespan.
#
# Without this tracking layer, the system would have no visibility
# into module behavior or task lifecycle boundaries.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 3 — LOOP DETECTION LOGIC
# ============================================================
# The engine monitors repeated interactions between module pairs.
#
# Example:
#   Module A → Module B → Module A → Module B
#
# If this pattern exceeds the configured loop threshold:
#   ttl_limits["loop"] = 3
#
# The engine flags the pair as a loop.
#
# LOOP DETECTION PURPOSE:
#   - Prevent infinite conversational cycles.
#   - Prevent modules from bouncing tasks endlessly.
#   - Prevent runaway autonomous reasoning loops.
#
# When a loop is detected:
#   - The event is logged.
#   - The orchestrator may intervene.
#   - Tasks may be terminated depending on severity.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 4 — TTL (TIME‑TO‑LIVE) ENFORCEMENT
# ============================================================
# Every task registered with the engine receives a timestamp.
#
# The TTL limit:
#   ttl_limits["task"] = 10 seconds
#
# If a task exceeds this lifespan:
#   - It is marked as expired.
#   - The orchestrator may kill the task.
#
# TTL PURPOSE:
#   - Prevent long‑running tasks from blocking the system.
#   - Prevent deadlocks.
#   - Ensure predictable execution windows.
#   - Maintain system responsiveness.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 5 — FORCED TERMINATION LOGIC
# ============================================================
# The engine can forcibly kill tasks when:
#   - TTL expires
#   - Loop detection triggers
#   - The orchestrator issues a kill signal
#   - A module becomes unresponsive
#
# KILL EFFECTS:
#   - Task timestamp removed
#   - Active task count decremented
#   - Kill event logged
#
# This mechanism ensures that no module can monopolize system
# resources or create deadlock conditions.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 6 — LOGGING & TELEMETRY
# ============================================================
# The engine logs:
#   - loops detected
#   - kills performed
#   - active task count
#   - timestamps
#
# HISTORY LIMIT:
#   limits["history"] = 200 entries
#
# Logs are used by:
#   - Stability & Recovery Engine
#   - System Orchestrator
#   - Meta‑Cognitive Reflection Engine
#
# Logging ensures traceability and post‑incident analysis.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 7 — FAILURE MODES & RECOVERY
# ============================================================
# 1. LOOP OVERFLOW
#    - Too many repeated interactions.
#    - Recovery: orchestrator resets interaction counters.
#
# 2. TTL EXPIRATION
#    - Task exceeds lifespan.
#    - Recovery: kill task.
#
# 3. DEADLOCK BETWEEN MODULES
#    - Two modules wait on each other.
#    - Recovery: kill both tasks.
#
# 4. RUNAWAY TASK SPAWNING
#    - Active task count grows abnormally.
#    - Recovery: orchestrator throttles module activity.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 8 — EXPANSION GUIDELINES
# ============================================================
# SAFE TO MODIFY:
#   - loop thresholds
#   - TTL values
#   - logging verbosity
#   - interaction pruning logic
#
# DO NOT MODIFY:
#   - core loop detection algorithm
#   - TTL expiration mechanism
#   - forced termination logic
#   - logging structure
#
# These components are foundational to system safety.


# ============================================================
# SECTION 9 — NOTES FOR TRUSTED ENGINEERS
# ============================================================
# - All autonomous modules must register tasks through this engine.
# - Never bypass guardrails for "quick tests".
# - Loop thresholds should be tuned carefully.
# - TTL values must reflect real hardware performance.
# - Logs should be reviewed during stabilization cycles.
#
# This engine is mandatory for safe multi‑agent orchestration.
